<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Old Great Bulgaria</h1><p class="st-meta">632 CE – 668 CE (approx.) · capital Phanagoria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Khan Kubrat's short-lived Bulgar state north of the Black Sea, whose break-up sent Bulgars to the Danube and the Volga.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 630s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a> threw off the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onogurs">Onogur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> tribes of the steppe between the Kuban and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. Raised partly at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and baptised there, he made an alliance with the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, who gave him the title of patrician.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Great Bulgaria was a confederation of Turkic-speaking nomadic tribes ruled by a khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan">Dulo clan</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanagoria">Phanagoria</a> on the Taman peninsula as a centre. Its wealth is shown by the treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereshchepina-treasure">Malaya Pereshchepina</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, often identified as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat's</a> burial, with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> vessels and Byzantine gifts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a> died around 665, his sons divided the people as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> pressure grew. Batbayan stayed and submitted to the Khazars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotrag">Kotrag</a> led his followers north to found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a> moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, where he founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> in 681.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/old-great-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Old Great Bulgaria" width="320" height="146"><figcaption>Image: Nk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Great_Bulgaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>632 CE – 668 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phanagoria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Organa, Kubrat, Batbayan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bulgar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Bulgaria, Magna Bulgaria, Onoguria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=650&amp;focus=old-great-bulgaria" aria-label="Old Great Bulgaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5658 497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Old Great Bulgaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5978 753L5653 895M5978 753L6820 913M5978 753L6131 811M5978 753L6076 554"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5653 895h0M6820 913h0M6131 811h0M6076 554h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5978 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5978 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=650&amp;focus=old-great-bulgaria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
